{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n1le02ob05re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n1le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n1le02ob05re2img01.jpg", "text": "<p><strong>Tom</strong>: Hey, Carol. What are you doing?</p>\n<p><strong>Carol</strong>: I am reading about the history of programming languages and operating systems. I have an oral presentation tomorrow. Actually, I think you can help me! Let's pretend you are my audience, OK?</p>\n<p><strong>Tom:</strong> Sure, go ahead!</p>\n<p><strong>Carol</strong>: Thanks. Here I go. Did you know that the word <i>software</i> wasn't used before 1958? Actually, at that time, the word <i>computer</i> was not related to electronic machines but to people who work out mathematical problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Tom</strong>: Wow. I didn't know that.</p>\n<p><strong>Carol</strong>: In fact, a mathematician called Grace Murray Hopper joined Eckert-Mauchly Computer Corporation to develop an instruction code for their famous computer UNIVAC. She used the term <i>automatic programming</i> to talk about her work.</p>\n<p><strong>Tom</strong>: Was that code the first programming language?</p>\n<p><strong>Carol</strong>: No, it wasn't, but her work pointed the way to the development of FORTRAN, which stands for <i>Formula Translator</i>. It was the world's first computer language. Workers from IBM developed it in 1954.</p>\n<p><strong>Tom</strong>: What did FORTRAN do?</p>\n<p><strong>Carol</strong>: FORTRAN was a language that made it possible for people to program computers using mathematical formulas and simple English-like instructions. It reduced costs in computer operations. Its development led to another important programming language known as COBOL (Common Business-Oriented Language), and then to a number of other third generation languages, such as ALGOL (Algorithmic Language), BASIC (Beginner&rsquo;s All-purpose Symbolic Instruction Code), or RPG (Report Program Generator).</p>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n1le02ob05re2img02.jpg", "text": "<p><strong>Tom</strong>: What are the characteristics of third generation computer languages?</p>\n<p><strong>Carol</strong>: This kind of languages was not only more efficient but also easier to learn. They became very popular as they standardized program design because of their English-like syntax. 3GLs enabled programs to be hierarchical and structured.</p>\n<p><strong>Tom</strong>: What about operating systems in the first electronic computers?</p>", "time": "123500"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n1le02ob05re2img03.jpg", "text": "<p><strong>Carol</strong>: Well, early computers did not have programming languages or operating systems. People had to individually program every function, and there was not any difference between systems and applications software. The first true operating system was MAD, which stands for <i>Michigan Algorithmic Decoder</i>. This operating system was developed at the University of Michigan in the late 50s. It derives from ALGOL; however, the concept of operating systems was not popular until the development of IBM's S/360.</p>\n<p><strong>Tom</strong>: Wow. That was actually a really fascinating presentation. I'm sure you'll do great!</p>", "time": "159000"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "img_path": "", "stimuli_ids": [0], "questions": [], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about the first computer languages. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can present characteristics for third generation computer languages. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can mention information about operating systems in the first electronic computers.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"c